\begin{abstract}
We compare multinomial logistic regression (LR) with $k$-nearest neighbours ($k$NN, $k$ chosen by inner
cross-validation) on the Iris data (OpenML dataset 61) by \nRep{} repetitions of stratified \nSplits-fold
cross-validation, on splits shared by both methods. With corrected resampled-$t$ 95\% intervals, LR reaches a mean
accuracy of \accLRMean\% [\accLRLo, \accLRHi] and $k$NN \accKNNMean\% [\accKNNLo, \accKNNHi]. The paired difference
LR$-$$k$NN is $\accDiffMean$ percentage points (pp) [$\accDiffLo$, $\accDiffHi$]: the interval contains zero, so
the study detects no accuracy difference between the two methods. Runs on an Ibex CPU node (\texttt{\ibexNode}) and locally gave byte-identical results.
\end{abstract}

\section{Data and protocol}
\textbf{Data.} The code downloads Iris \citep{fisher1936} from OpenML \citep{vanschoren2013openml} (dataset 61,
version 1, ARFF, md5 \texttt{\dataMDFive}, equal to OpenML's checksum): 150 flowers, 4 measurements in cm, 3 classes
of 50. It is used as downloaded; \nDuplicateRows{} rows repeat an earlier row exactly.

\textbf{Methods.} Both methods are scikit-learn pipelines that standardize the features on the training data only.
LR is a single softmax (multinomial) model fitted by L-BFGS with an L2 penalty and fixed $C=1$ (not tuned). $k$NN
uses Euclidean distance and uniform weights. $k\in\{\kGridMin,\dots,\kGridMax\}$ is chosen by stratified 5-fold
cross-validation \emph{inside each outer training set}, with ties going to the smallest $k$.

\textbf{Protocol and intervals.} \texttt{RepeatedStratifiedKFold} (\nSplits{} folds, \nRep{} repetitions, seed
\seed) gives $J=\nFolds$ outer splits of $n_\text{train}=\nTrain$ and $n_\text{test}=\nTest$ flowers. Both methods
are scored on the same splits, so the \nFolds{} per-fold accuracy differences are paired. Fold scores share training
data, so a $t$-interval treating them as independent is too narrow. We use the
corrected resampled $t$ \citep{nadeau2003inference,bouckaert2004evaluating},
$\bar m \pm t_{0.975,J-1}\sqrt{(1/J + n_\text{test}/n_\text{train})\,s^2}$ with $t_{0.975,49}=\tCrit$, for each
method's fold accuracies and for the paired differences.

