\section{Reproducibility}
One code base with numpy \verNumpy, scipy \verScipy{} and scikit-learn \verSklearn{} (Python \verPythonIbex{} on
Ibex, \verPython{} locally) ran the full cross-validation once as a SLURM CPU job on Ibex (job \ibexJobId, partition
\texttt{\ibexPartition}, node \texttt{\ibexNode}, recorded inside the job from \texttt{SLURMD\_NODENAME}) and once
locally. The criterion was byte identity of the per-fold results file and the per-case predictions file, plus exact
equality of every stored value. The two runs are identical: \runsDifferingFolds{} of \nFolds{} folds and
\runsDifferingPredictions{} of \nPredictionsCompared{} predictions differ. The numbers above are from the local run.
The code is in \texttt{report/code/}; the logs and per-fold outputs of both runs are in \texttt{report/results/}.

\section{Conclusion and limitations}
On Iris under this protocol, multinomial logistic regression and $k$NN with tuned $k$ are both about 95\% accurate,
and their paired difference is not distinguishable from zero. This is one small dataset near the accuracy ceiling,
so nothing here generalizes beyond it. The corrected resampled $t$ is an approximation, and the duplicate rows
slightly favour nearest-neighbour methods. The NeurIPS paper checklist is omitted to keep this note to two pages.

